    researchContext: "Population epidemiology places IQ 40–45 in the moderate-to-severe disability range, with prevalence under 0.5% (McLaren & Bryson, 1987, British Journal of Psychiatry). The AAIDD supports-based model (Thompson et al., 2009) demonstrates that adaptive behavior — not IQ — predicts quality of life outcomes. Schalock et al. (2012) found adults in this range who received consistent person-centered support achieved greater community integration than those receiving institutional care. Research on neuroplasticity confirms that enriched environments and early intervention produce measurable cognitive gains even at this level.",
