    flynnContext: "A score of 109 today corresponds to approximately IQ 97–100 on 1950 norms. The one-point gap from the IQ 110 high-average threshold is entirely artificial — both profiles represent the same cognitive system and predict identical outcomes in all population studies. The Flynn Effect means that today's person at IQ 109 possesses substantially greater raw cognitive capacity than someone who scored IQ 109 on 1950 norms, reflecting genuine improvements in human cognitive development across generations.",
